# ConContexto — environment variable reference
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in the secrets:
#
#     cp env.example .env
#
# Docker Compose reads `.env` from the repository root automatically.
# `.env` is git-ignored; this file is committed and MUST stay in sync with
# what the code actually reads (see "Keeping this file honest" at the end).
#
# Every variable below was traced from source, not assumed:
#   POSTGRES_*      -> docker-compose.yml, scripts/backup/pg_dump.sh
#   DATABASE_URL    -> app/cmd/concontexto/migrate_cmd.go
#   PORT            -> app/cmd/concontexto/serve.go, app/internal/healthcheck
#   STATIC_ROOT     -> app/cmd/concontexto/serve.go
#   POSTGRES_ADDR   -> app/internal/healthcheck
#   APP_DATA_ROOT   -> app/cmd/concontexto/ingest_cmd.go
#   APP_SCHEDULE_INTERVAL -> app/cmd/concontexto/schedule.go


# =============================================================================
# REQUIRED — deployment fails without this
# =============================================================================

# PostgreSQL superuser password for the stack's database.
# docker-compose.yml declares this with `:?` so Compose REFUSES to start
# rather than silently booting with an empty password. There is deliberately
# no default. Set it in the Portainer stack environment or in `.env`, never
# in a committed file.
#
# Generate a strong value with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=


# =============================================================================
# DATABASE — defaults match docker-compose.yml
# =============================================================================

# Database name and role. The backup script (scripts/backup/pg_dump.sh)
# defaults to these same values, so overriding one means overriding it for
# backups too.
POSTGRES_DB=concontexto
POSTGRES_USER=concontexto

# Connection string used by the `migrate` subcommand and, from slice 5a
# onwards, by the ingestion adapters.
#
# Inside the Compose stack this is BUILT AUTOMATICALLY from POSTGRES_USER /
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD / POSTGRES_DB — you do NOT need to set it there.
# Set it only when running the binary or the tests from the host, pointed at
# a database you reached some other way.
#
# `sslmode=disable` is acceptable for the in-stack connection ONLY because
# PRD §14.3 puts Postgres on the `internal` Docker network with no published
# ports: it is unreachable from the host and from Nginx Proxy Manager. If
# that ever changes, this must change with it.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://concontexto:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/concontexto?sslmode=disable

# Host:port used by `healthcheck --deep` for a credential-free TCP
# reachability probe. Deliberately separate from DATABASE_URL: the shallow
# probe must stay usable when no credentials are configured.
POSTGRES_ADDR=postgres:5432


# =============================================================================
# APPLICATION RUNTIME — the image already sets sane defaults
# =============================================================================

# HTTP listen port. Baked into the image as ENV PORT=8080; override only to
# run on a different port. Nginx Proxy Manager reaches the container by name
# on this port over the shared `proxy` network — no port is published to the
# host.
PORT=8080

# Directory the static handler serves from. Baked into the image as
# ENV STATIC_ROOT=/web/dist, where the `public_html` volume is mounted.
# The Go default is the RELATIVE path `web/dist`, which is why the image
# sets the absolute one explicitly — do not rely on the working directory.
# Override only when running `serve` from a source checkout.
STATIC_ROOT=/web/dist

# Directory the `ingest` subcommand archives raw downloads and the raw-
# file hash listing under (spec raw-file-archive; PRD §14.2). The Go
# default is the fixed path `/app_data` — docker-compose.yml's own
# `app_data` volume mount — so this normally needs no override inside the
# Compose stack. Override only when running `ingest --series`/`--source`
# from a source checkout, mirroring STATIC_ROOT's own convention.
# APP_DATA_ROOT=/app_data

# How long `serve`'s in-process scheduler (verify-report CRITICAL C4) waits
# after a SUCCESSFUL scheduled cycle for a source before running it again.
# A Go duration string (e.g. "24h", "30m"). The Go default is 24h,
# matching PRD §9.2's publication-cadence language. A FAILED cycle instead
# retries at the scheduler's own backoff, never this interval. An unset
# or unparsable value falls back to the default and is logged, never fatal.
# APP_SCHEDULE_INTERVAL=24h


# =============================================================================
# OPERATIONS — optional
# =============================================================================

# Compose service name of the PostgreSQL container, used by
# scripts/backup/pg_dump.sh. Change only if the service is renamed.
# COMPOSE_POSTGRES_SERVICE=postgres

# Compose project name. Useful to run an isolated stack (a smoke test, a
# staging instance) beside the real one without colliding on volume or
# container names.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=concontexto


# =============================================================================
# NOT USED YET — reserved by the roadmap, listed so nobody re-invents them
# =============================================================================
#
# Nothing here is read by any code yet. Each arrives with its slice:
#   slice 5a  raw-file archive path, if it stops being the fixed /app_data mount
#   slice 9   alerting destination for failed ingestion / failed validation
#   Fase 3    public API key store and rate-limit configuration (PRD §11.4)
#
# Add them here in the SAME commit that first reads them, never later.


# =============================================================================
# Keeping this file honest
# =============================================================================
#
# The DATABASE_URL bug proved that deployment wiring is an integration
# surface with NO unit-test coverage: every Go test passed while the compose
# file was missing a variable the binary required, so `migrate up` would have
# silently done nothing on the VPS.
#
# The guard against a repeat is `scripts/check-env-example.sh`, run in CI: it
# fails the build when a name passed to os.Getenv / os.LookupEnv anywhere in
# the Go source is absent from this file. Documentation that CI enforces is
# documentation that stays true.
